\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless stated otherwise, results are for Bernoulli assignment and $\sigma=0.5$ on the synthetic graphs of Section~\ref{sec:design}; intervals are Monte Carlo intervals with graph draws as the unit of replication.

\subsection{Variance follows leverage; bias does not}
\label{sec:varbias}

\paragraph{Variance.}
The sampling variance of the regression estimator follows $\sigma^2L/n$. Where the specification is correct (the plain SBM at $\sigma=0.5$, the no-spillover column and mechanism (a) at $s=\tfrac12$; the 42 cells of the $n$, $d$ and $p$ slices), log SD falls with $n$ with slope $-0.50$, rises with $1+d$ with slope $+0.55$ [0.547, 0.556] and falls with $p(1-p)$ with slope $-0.50$ (predicted: $\pm\tfrac12$; Figure~\ref{fig:var}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}). The $d$ slope is not $\tfrac12$ because realised leverage scales as $L\propto(1+d)^{1.09}$ over 210 graph draws.

\paragraph{Bias.}
The population-OLS prediction of the bias (Eq.~\ref{eq:bias}; exact in form, it uses the true response and the realised graph) lies inside the Monte Carlo interval of the measured bias (Figure~\ref{fig:bias}) in 85.1\% of 1224 cells, and in 86.7\% with the finite-$n$ centring of the fit (added post hoc). The median absolute bias over $p$ and $s$ varies by under 15\% across $d=5,20,80$ for the diffusion and interaction mechanisms (e), (f); it rises for the saturating mechanism (c) from 0.19 to 0.33 and for the threshold mechanism (d) from 0.17 to 0.45--0.48. The bias of the count mechanism (b) equals $s\,[\mathrm{HM}(d_i)/\mathrm{AM}(d_i)-1]$ (correlation 0.999 over 60 runs; exact for tree-like neighbourhoods, untested beyond), so it falls with $d$ where degrees are near-Poisson (SBM, ER: 0.115, 0.025, 0.007 at $d=5,20,80$ on the SBM) and not on Barab\'asi--Albert ($-0.12$ to $-0.16$), Stanford3 ($-0.26$ to $-0.44$) or ego-Facebook ($-0.27$ to $-0.41$). For the saturating mechanism the bias changes sign at $p=0.397$ [0.393, 0.401] at $d=20$ on a grid of step 0.05, where the population-OLS prediction, which uses the true response, is 0.397, as the curvature term predicts; this tests the extrapolation account and does not locate the bias-free $p$ for a practitioner (Figure~\ref{fig:pdep}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}: $d=5$, 80).
