\subsection{Where regression wins and where it loses}
\label{sec:regime}

The regression has smaller RMSE than DM whenever the spillover share is large enough for DM's bias $-s\gate$ to exceed the regression's error (Figure~\ref{fig:regime}; ``wins'' is the paired interval below zero). Over the grid of this study (Table~\ref{tab:regime}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}) regression wins in 79.6\%, 77.8\%, 69.9\%, 55.8\% and 31.3\% of cells at $\sigma=0.25,0.5,1,2,4$ ($\sigma=2,4$ are post-freeze extensions). At $\sigma=0.5$ it is 0.49 for the threshold mechanism (d) and 0.75--0.87 for the others. The break-even spillover share $s^*$ at $d=20$ is about 0.19 for (a), (b), (c), (e), 0.25 for (f), and does not exist for (d), where regression loses at every share on every synthetic graph.

The failure on the threshold mechanism does not transfer to the thinned real graphs: regression wins on (d) at all three degrees (log ratio $-1.19$ to $-0.33$ on ego-Facebook, $-1.15$ to $-0.89$ on Stanford3) and in every other real-graph row except (b) at $d=80$ on ego-Facebook, where it wins in two of three shares. The cause is not diagnosed. At $d\approx20$, $p=\tfrac12$ the real graphs are not nearer the threshold (share of exposures within $\pm0.15$ of it 0.56--0.57, synthetic 0.64--0.68) but more spread (SD 0.20--0.21, synthetic 0.11--0.13), with about a third of the bias (0.20--0.25, synthetic 0.68--0.77). A degree tail (Barab\'asi--Albert), clustering (Watts--Strogatz) or both (Holme--Kim) does not reproduce it, and log-normal degrees only part of the way at $d=20$; a wide exposure spread goes with the smaller bias at $d=20$ but not at $d=80$ (exploratory; Appendix~\ref{app:extra}).
