\paragraph{Can the regime be read off the data?}
A rule that recommends regression when its own estimate of the spillover share, $\hat s=1-\hat\beta_z/\hat\tau$, exceeds its HC0 standard error is right in 91.8\% [89.3, 93.8] of 1440 cells; here a win is a point estimate of the log ratio below zero. Always using regression is right in 80.2\% [74.7, 85.4] of these cells, so the rule adds 12 points [7, 16], at the price of recommending regression wrongly in about a quarter of the cells where DM is better.

\paragraph{Flexible learners do not remove the problem.}
On 12--14 cells per mechanism at 200 draws per graph (Figure~\ref{fig:tiers}), none of lasso, ridge, random forest, gradient boosting and a simplified adaptive-threshold H\'ajek estimator has a smaller median RMSE than the regression on mechanisms (a), (b), (c), (e), (f): the five learners' median ratios there are 1.6--3.8. On the threshold mechanism (d), boosting, forests and the adaptive H\'ajek estimator cannot be told apart from it (median ratios 0.99 [0.70, 1.18], 1.09 [0.76, 1.59], 1.02 [0.59, 1.73]); lasso and ridge are twice it. A small untuned graph neural network (13 cells, 50 draws) is the best learner, yet its RMSE exceeds the regression's in 66 of 91 cell--mechanism rows (paired interval above zero) and is smaller in 5, all on (d).
