\subsection{Graph-cluster randomisation}
\label{sec:cluster}

Cluster designs move the exposures towards the corners, which shrinks the extrapolation and the bias, and reduce the effective sample to about the number of clusters. At $d=20$ the regression under a cluster design has a lower RMSE than the Bernoulli regression in 68 of 78 graph--mechanism--clustering comparisons and than cluster DM in 62, and than a H\'ajek estimator in 68\% of 132 further cells (Appendix~\ref{app:extra}); for the threshold mechanism (d) it beats cluster DM in 2 of 16. Cluster-bootstrap intervals cover in 83\% of 396 cluster cells (Figure~\ref{fig:cluster}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}).

\subsection{Confidence intervals do not reach nominal coverage}
\label{sec:coverage}

The 95\% HC0 interval of the regression covers the true GATE in 73.6\% of the 1800 Bernoulli cells of Figure~\ref{fig:cov}. Coverage is set by the size of the bias relative to the SD and not by leverage: the mean is 93\%, 92\%, 87\%, 67\% and 16\% for $|{\rm bias}|/{\rm SD}$ in $\le0.25$, 0.25--0.5, 0.5--1, 1--2 and $>2$.

An interval widened by a bound on the unidentified part \citep{armstrong2020simple} covers only by being wide: on the 666 block cells it covers 0.99 (HC0 at 90\%: 0.70) at 8--14 times the width of HC0 (by stratum of $|{\rm bias}|/{\rm SD}$), no better than HC0 inflated to the same width (coverage difference $-0.010$, $[-0.020, -0.002]$ over 24 groups). Most of the width is noise in the estimated curvature (7, 25, 109 at $d=5$, 20, 80 with the response removed; true value at most 4.7). Using the true curvature (infeasible) the interval has at most about the oracle's width ($\kappa=0.25$, the grid floor) and covers 0.91 of the block's smooth cells (uniform inflation: 0.90; the original cells do not reach 0.90) but 0.59 of (b) and (e), whose bias is not curvature; a feasible estimate's cost is not measured (exploratory, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}). On the threshold mechanism outside the observed exposures, where we expected failure, it covers 0.998 of 27 cells.
